# Third-party source material — kept locally, not redistributed.
# GRAMMAR_LAB was built from this file; the 35 verbs it contains are recorded
# in GRAMMAR_LAB/data/verbs.js, so nothing in the site depends on it being here.
English-Telugu-+RootVerbs.pdf
*.pdf

# Private material. The conversation transcripts are recordings of family at home: they
# contain relatives' names, financial disputes, health details and ages. None of those
# people agreed to be published, and this repo is public. Only de-identified linguistic
# output derived from them may ever be committed.
sources/private/

# macOS
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# Editor / tooling
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Python bytecode from anki/tools
__pycache__/
*.pyc
audio/

# Reader pages baked from private sources — same rule as sources/private itself.
STUDY/data/reader/private-*.js

# Third-party content kept for personal study, not redistributed. The podcast is a copyrighted
# YouTube episode: the audio alone is 105 MB (over GitHub's per-file limit anyway) and the full
# transcript is the whole work. Both stay local; only the tooling that reads them is committed.
sources/local/
STUDY/data/reader/local-*.js

# The Harry Potter translation project. Two separate reasons, and the second is the one people
# forget:
#   source/ is the English novel, in copyright.
#   work/   is our Telugu translation of it. A translation is a *derivative work* — it is no
#           more publishable than the original, and Rowling's rights holders have a long record
#           of pursuing exactly this. This repo is public, so the translation never lands in it.
# What IS committed: the tooling, the style guide, the address matrix and the glossary. Those
# are our own analysis and a term list, not the book. If work/ is ever lost, everything needed
# to redo it consistently survives in the committed half.
translate/source/
translate/work/

# LingQ Mini Stories. The English source is LingQ's, pulled under a personal account key or
# pasted by hand; neither belongs in a public repo. Our Telugu translation in ministories/work/
# IS committed — LingQ released these for communities to translate into languages they do not
# support, and redistributing the translation is the whole point. See ministories/README.md.
ministories/source/

# Scratch export/import files for the HyperTTS audio path — regenerated from work/*.tsv, not a
# source of truth. See ministories/README.md "Audio via HyperTTS".
ministories/hypertts_export.tsv
ministories/*.export.tsv

# Continuous per-story audio + SRT tracks (tools/ms_lr_export.py) are COMMITTED, reversing the
# earlier ignore: the reader streams lr/*.mp3, GitHub Pages serves only what is committed, and
# the phone can't play audio that isn't hosted. It is our own Azure TTS of our own translation
# (~2 MB/story), so nothing third-party ships. The per-segment clips below stay local — they
# are the regenerable intermediates, not what the site plays.

# A raw Anki plain-text export, dropped here mid-session as the intermediate artifact of the
# HyperTTS audio path. Already consumed by tools/ms_hypertts_import.py; redundant with
# ministories/work/*.tsv from here on. Anki's own default deck/export name, not ours.
ministories/Default.txt

# Per-word pronunciation clips, un-ignored on the same reasoning as ministories/lr/: our own TTS
# of our own translation, and GitHub Pages can only serve what is committed — the reader's
# speaker button is dead on the phone otherwise. ~2.9 MB for stories 1-5's 267 words; distinct
# words grow sublinearly, so the full 60 stories should land near 25-30 MB.
# Three lines, because git will not descend into an excluded directory: un-exclude the parent,
# re-exclude the per-segment clips directly inside it, then re-include only words/.
!ministories/audio/
ministories/audio/*
!ministories/audio/words/

# Anki's own export filename for the word-audio round trip — same scratch status as Default.txt.
# Already consumed by ms_hypertts_import.py --words; the clips in audio/words/ are the artifact.
ministories/Telugu__*.txt
